// ALG1 Unit 04 Lesson 08 Guided Practice
// Lesson: Putting It All Together
// MLA Standards: MLA.A1.FN.1; MLA.A1.FN.2; MLA.A1.FN.3; MLA.A1.FN.4; MLA.A1.LR.4; MLA.A1.AR.8

::ALG1_U04_L08_GP_Q01::
Question ID: ALG1_U04_L08_GP_Q01

MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.1

Question:
Which relation is a function?
{
~A. (1,2),(1,3),(2,4)#Input 1 has two different outputs.
=B. (1,2),(2,2),(3,2)#Correct. Each input has exactly one output.
~C. (4,5),(4,6),(5,7)#Input 4 has two different outputs.
~D. (0,1),(0,1),(0,2)#Input 0 is paired with different outputs.
}

::ALG1_U04_L08_GP_Q02::
Question ID: ALG1_U04_L08_GP_Q02

MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.2

Question:
If f(x)=2x^2+1, what is f(3)?
{
~A. 13#This doubles 3 first and then squares incorrectly.
~B. 17#This is close, but it leaves out the final +1 after 2(9).
~C. 10#This squares 3 and adds 1 but misses multiplying by 2.
=D. 19#Correct. f(3)=2(9)+1=19.
}

::ALG1_U04_L08_GP_Q03::
Question ID: ALG1_U04_L08_GP_Q03

MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.3

Question:
A graph crosses the x-axis at x=-4. What feature is shown?
{
=A. zero#Correct. A zero is an input where the output is 0.
~B. y-intercept#A y-intercept occurs when x = 0.
~C. maximum#An x-axis crossing does not necessarily show the greatest output.
~D. rate of change#A crossing point is not a rate of change.
}

::ALG1_U04_L08_GP_Q04::
Question ID: ALG1_U04_L08_GP_Q04

MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.8

Question:
What is the minimum value of y=|x+3|?
{
~A. -3#Absolute value outputs cannot be negative.
~B. 3#The graph is centered at x = -3, but the minimum output is 0.
=C. 0#Correct. The smallest possible distance is 0.
~D. 6#This can be an output, but it is not the minimum.
}

::ALG1_U04_L08_GP_Q05::
Question ID: ALG1_U04_L08_GP_Q05

MLA Standard: MLA.A1.LR.4

Question:
Function A is A(x)=5x+2. Function B is B(x)=3x+10. Which function has the greater rate of change?
{
~A. Function B#Function B has slope 3.
=B. Function A#Correct. Function A has slope 5, which is greater than 3.
~C. They have the same rate#The slopes are different.
~D. Cannot be determined#The rates are shown by the coefficients of x.
}

